          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have both asthma and reflux and my cough is worse at night. Which is more likely to be driving it, and what does guidance describe for each?",
          "answer": "It’s important to discuss your symptoms with a healthcare provider to determine if your asthma or gastroesophageal reflux disease (GERD) is more significantly contributing to your worsening cough at night. \n\nAsthma is known for causing nighttime cough, which can be exacerbated by various triggers. Management typically includes medications like inhaled corticosteroids and bronchodilators, avoiding triggers, regular monitoring, and making lifestyle changes (Diagnostic document on asthma). \n\nConversely, GERD can lead to coughing due to acid irritating the throat, particularly when lying down. Management usually involves a trial of proton pump inhibitors, making lifestyle changes like weight reduction, and dietary modifications (Research document on GERD). \n\nBoth conditions can influence your nighttime cough, and it may be necessary to explore each factor with your clinician to tailor an effective management strategy for your situation.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
